// A generic adds its `T` only if its bound says `T` adds: an unbounded parameter promises nothing, so `x + x` is
// refused where the generic is declared, with the bound to write — even though `main` calls it with an `int32`,
// which adds. (tests/arith_bound has it written.)
fn T twice<T>(T x) { return x + x; }
fn uint8 main() { return cast<uint8>(twice(x: 21)); }
